13:26:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's fair pushback, and you're right that this isn't something you invented—there's actual research suggesting some average differences in early self-regulation between boys and girls, particularly around ages 3-6. Girls' brains and nervous systems do tend, on average, to develop certain impulse-control and verbal-processing skills slightly earlier. So the pattern you and other parents are noticing isn't imaginary. **But here's where I'd still add nuance:** 1. **"Average difference" doesn't mean "your son."** Even where group differences exist, the overlap between boys and girls is huge—there are plenty of boys who are calmer and more focused than plenty of girls. Knowing the average tells you very little about where your specific kid falls. 2. **Observer bias is real.** When parents and teachers expect boys to be more disruptive, they often notice and comment on boys' fidgeting more than girls', and sometimes girls' quieter struggles (anxiety, inattentiveness) get overlooked because it doesn't match the "girls sit still" script. So some of what's driving the perception is real, but some is also selective attention. 3. **The label can become self-fulfilling.** If a boy hears from age 3 that "boys are wild" or "boys don't listen," it can shape how he sees himself and how much effort he puts into self-control—versus a kid who's just told "you're the kind of person who tries hard to focus." **Practically speaking for your son:** Instead of bracing for "boy behavior," it's probably more useful to just observe *him* specifically once he's in class—his teachers will have useful, individualized feedback. If he does turn out to be more high-energy or impulsive, that's totally manageable with the usual tools: clear routines, physical outlets, positive reinforcement for the behaviors you want to see. So: not baseless, but also not a life sentence for your particular kid.
