13:27:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's the key logical question, so let me be precise about it: it's not that your son would be a "magical exception"—it's that group patterns and individual predictions are just different kinds of claims, even when the group pattern is completely real and true. Here's a clean way to see why: **Height is a good parallel.** Men are taller than women *on average*—that's a real, well-documented pattern, not a stereotype. But if I told you "there's a man over there," you couldn't conclude "therefore he's taller than that specific woman standing next to him." Plenty of individual women are taller than plenty of individual men, even though the average holds. Knowing someone's sex tells you something about the distribution they're drawn from, but very little about where exactly they land in it—especially if the average difference is modest and the distributions overlap a lot (which they do for both height and self-regulation). **Maturity/self-regulation works the same way.** Even if boys as a group average, say, 6 months behind girls in some self-regulation measure at age 5, that's a small average difference relative to the *range* of variation within each sex. Some individual boys are ahead of most girls. Some individual girls are behind most boys. The group-level fact is true and the individual-level uncertainty is also true—these aren't in tension. **Why this matters practically for you:** if you walk into this assuming "he will struggle because he's a boy," you might miss actual signals about *him*—like if he's naturally more focused than that pattern predicts, you don't want to over-manage or under-trust him based on a stat that may not apply to his specific case. And if he does struggle, it's more useful to address his specific behaviors than to chalk it up to "boys will be boys," because that framing can sometimes make both parents and teachers less rigorous about helping him build the skills he needs. So: the pattern is real, you're not wrong to expect it's *more likely* on average, but "more likely on average" and "definitely true of my son" are different claims, and only observing him directly will tell you which one applies.
